{
  "dom_id": "step-629524",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Writing and Reading Formal Notation",
  "problem": "Let $S$ be a set and let $f$ be a real-valued function on $S.$ Consider the expression\n\n$$\nx^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x).\n$$\n\nWhat does this statement mean in words? Also, what does $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ represent?",
  "worked_solution": "The expression $x^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ means that:\n\n- $x^*$ is an input value in $S.$\n\n- Among all $x \\in S,$ the value $f(x^*)$ is as large as possible.\n\nIn other words, $x^*$ is a choice of input where $f$ attains its largest value on $S.$\n\nBy contrast, $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is not an input. It is the largest *function value* that $f$ attains on $S.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
